ons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"99934803":{"pageid":99934803,"ns":6,"title":"File:Fmars-08-642040.pdf","index":7,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4360403,"width":1239,"height":1622,"pagecount":11,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Fmars-08-642040.pdf/page1-960px-Fmars-08-642040.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":2356,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Fmars-08-642040.pdf/page1-960px-Fmars-08-642040.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":2356},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Fmars-08-642040.pdf?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fmars-08-642040.pdf","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=99934803","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"The seafloor beneath floating ice shelves accounts roughly a third of the Antarctic\u2019s 5 million km2 of continental shelf. Prior to this study, our knowledge of these habitats and the life they support was restricted to what has been observed from eight boreholes drilled for geological and glaciological studies. The established theory of sub-ice shelf biogeography is that both functional and taxonomic diversities decrease along a nutrient gradient with distance from the ice shelf front, resulting in a depauperate fauna, dominated by mobile scavengers and predators toward the grounding line. Mobile macro-benthic life and mega-benthic life have been observed as far as 700 km under an ice shelf. New observations from two boreholes in the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf challenge the idea that sessile organisms reduce in prevalence the further under the ice you go. The discovery of an established community consisting of only sessile, probably filter feeding, organisms (sponges and other taxa) on a boulder 260 km from the ice front raises significant questions, especially when the local currents suggest that this community is somewhere between 625 km and 1500 km in the direction of water flow from the nearest region of photosynthesis. This new evidence requires us to rethink our ideas with regard to the diversity of community types found under ice shelves, the key factors which control their distribution and their vulnerability to environmental change and ice shelf collapse.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2021-02-15","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"Huw J. Griffiths, Paul Anker, Katrin Linse, Jamie Maxwell, Alexandra L. Post, Craig Stevens, Slawek Tulaczyk and James A. Smith","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"149478529":{"pageid":149478529,"ns":6,"title":"File:Geomorphological evidence of large vertebrates interacting with the seafloor at abyssal depths in a region designated for deep-sea mining.pdf","index":2,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":1326304,"width":1085,"height":1575,"pagecount":10,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Geomorphological_evidence_of_large_vertebrates_interacting_with_the_seafloor_at_abyssal_depths_in_a_region_designated_for_deep-sea_mining.pdf/page1-960px-Geomorphological_evidence_of_large_vertebrates_interacting_with_the_seafloor_at_abyssal_depths_in_a_region_designated_for_deep-sea_mining.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":2613,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Geomorphological_evidence_of_large_vertebrates_interacting_with_the_seafloor_at_abyssal_depths_in_a_region_d